AI_TECH = {
"py:assignment": "Assignment statements are used to bind names to values and to modify attributes or items of mutable objects. When a target is a simple name, the name is bound to the object produced by evaluating the right-hand side in the current namespace. If the target is an attribute reference, the object's attribute is set, and if it is a subscription, the container's item assignment method is invoked. In chained assignment, the expression is evaluated once and then assigned to each target from left to right.",
"py:binary": "The binary arithmetic operators have the conventional priority levels. The multiplication operator yields the product of its arguments, which must either both be numbers or one must be an integer and the other a sequence. In the latter case, the sequence is repeated that many times. The division operators yield the quotient of their arguments, and dividing by zero raises a ZeroDivisionError exception. The modulo operator yields the remainder of the division of the first argument by the second.",
"py:class": "A class definition is an executable statement that creates a new class object. The inheritance list, if present, is evaluated first and normally gives a list of base classes. The class body is then executed in a new namespace, and when the body finishes, its namespace is passed to the metaclass, which constructs the class. Finally, the resulting class object is bound to the class name in the enclosing scope. Decorators, if specified, are applied to the class after it has been created.",
"py:comparisons": "Comparison operators can be chained arbitrarily, so that an expression such as x < y <= z is equivalent to x < y and y <= z, except that y is evaluated only once. If the first comparison is false, the remaining comparisons are not evaluated. The equality operators compare the values of two objects, whereas the identity operators test whether two names refer to exactly the same object. Objects of different types, except for different numeric types, never compare equal by default.",
"py:compound": "Compound statements contain groups of other statements and affect or control the execution of those other statements in some way. A compound statement consists of one or more clauses, each of which has a header and a suite. The header begins with a unique keyword and ends with a colon, and the suite is the group of statements controlled by the clause. A suite can be a set of statements on the same line as the header or one or more indented statements on subsequent lines.",
"py:context-managers": "A context manager is an object that defines the runtime context to be entered and exited around a block of code. It is invoked by the with statement, which calls the object's enter method before the suite is executed and its exit method afterward. The exit method receives information about any exception raised in the suite, and if it returns a true value, the exception is suppressed. Context managers are commonly used to ensure that resources such as files and locks are released reliably.",
"py:customization": "Classes can customize the behavior of built-in operations by defining special methods. The rich comparison methods, for example, correspond to the six comparison operators and are called to implement them. Each method receives the other operand and may return any value, though a Boolean is typical. If a method does not support the operation for the given operands, it should return the NotImplemented singleton, which signals the interpreter to try the reflected operation on the other operand.",
"py:debugger": "Python ships with an interactive debugger, pdb, that lets you pause a running program and look around. You can place breakpoints at chosen lines, advance one statement at a time, print variables, and move up and down the call stack to see how a function was reached. Calling the built-in breakpoint function in your code starts the debugger at that spot. If a program crashes, you can also start the debugger afterward and examine the state at the moment of failure.",
"py:dict": "Dictionaries map keys to values and are written with curly braces containing key and value pairs. Looking up a key is fast, because the key is hashed to locate its entry directly. Only hashable objects, such as strings, numbers, and tuples of immutable items, can serve as keys, whereas lists cannot. When the same key is written twice in one literal, the later pair silently replaces the earlier one. Since Python 3.7, dictionaries also remember the order in which keys were inserted.",
"py:dynamic-features": "Because Python resolves most names dynamically, several built-in functions allow code to be constructed and executed at runtime. The eval function evaluates an expression string, while the exec function executes a string containing statements. Both accept optional dictionaries that serve as the global and local namespaces for the evaluated code. If these arguments are omitted, the code is executed in the current namespace. Such features should be used with caution, particularly with untrusted input.",
"py:exceptions": "Exceptions provide a mechanism for transferring control out of the normal flow of a program when an error or other exceptional condition occurs. An exception is raised explicitly by the raise statement or implicitly by the interpreter when an operation fails. The try statement specifies exception handlers for a block of code, and the first matching except clause handles the exception. If no handler is found in the current frame, the exception propagates up the call stack until it is caught or terminates the program.",
"py:execmodel": "Python programs are built from code blocks, and each block has its own scope for names. Assigning to a name inside a function makes it local to that function unless a global or nonlocal declaration says otherwise. When a name is used, the interpreter looks in the local scope, then in any enclosing function scopes, then in the module's global scope, and finally in the built-in names. This lookup order is determined by the program text, not by the order in which calls happen at runtime.",
"py:for": "The for statement is used to iterate over the elements of a sequence or other iterable object. The expression list is evaluated once and must yield an iterable object, from which an iterator is created. The suite is then executed once for each item provided by the iterator, with the target list assigned the item in turn. When the items are exhausted, the optional else clause is executed, unless the loop was terminated by a break statement.",
"py:formatstrings": "The format string syntax uses replacement fields surrounded by curly braces. Anything that is not contained in braces is treated as literal text and copied unchanged to the output. A replacement field begins with an optional field name that specifies the object whose value is to be formatted, followed by an optional conversion and an optional format specification introduced by a colon. Literal braces can be included in the output by doubling them.",
"py:function": "A function definition is an executable statement that creates a function object and binds it to the function name in the current local namespace. The function body is not executed at definition time, but only when the function is called. Parameters can have default values, which are evaluated once, from left to right, when the function is defined. For this reason, using a mutable object such as a list as a default value can lead to unexpected behavior across calls.",
"py:import": "The import statement is executed in two steps: first it finds and loads the module, and then it binds the results to a name in the local namespace. If the requested module is not found in the module cache, the import system searches for it using a sequence of finders, which consult the entries of the module search path. Once loaded, the module object is stored in the cache so that subsequent imports of the same name return the same object.",
"py:lists": "Lists are ordered, mutable collections that can hold items of any type, including other lists. They are created with square brackets, either by writing the items out separated by commas or by using a comprehension that builds the contents from an iterable. Because lists are mutable, their items can be replaced, appended, or removed after creation, and several names may refer to the same list object. Indexing starts at zero, negative indices count from the end, and slicing returns a new list containing a copy of the selected range.",
"py:objects": "Every object has an identity, a type, and a value. The identity of an object never changes once it has been created, and it can be thought of as the object's address in memory. An object's type determines the operations that it supports and the possible values it can hold. Objects whose value can change are said to be mutable, while objects whose value cannot change once they are created are called immutable. Lists and dictionaries are mutable, whereas tuples and strings are immutable.",
"py:operator-summary": "The following summary lists the operators in order of precedence, from the lowest binding to the highest. Operators in the same group have the same precedence and, unless the syntax is explicitly given, are grouped from left to right. Exponentiation is an exception, as it groups from right to left. Parentheses may be used to override the default precedence and to make the intended grouping of an expression explicit for the reader.",
"py:sequence-types": "A class can emulate a sequence type by implementing the methods that the interpreter calls for container operations. The subscription method is called to evaluate an item lookup, and for sequence types the accepted keys should be integers and slice objects. Negative indices are typically handled by the method itself, and it should raise an IndexError for values outside the range of the sequence. Implementing the length method allows the built-in len function to work with the object.",
"py:slicings": "A slicing selects a range of items in a sequence object, such as a string, tuple, or list. Slicings may be used as expressions or as targets in assignment statements. The syntax consists of a primary followed by a square-bracketed slice list. A simple slice specifies a lower bound, an upper bound, and an optional stride, separated by colons. The resulting object has the same type as the original, and omitted bounds default to the beginning and end of the sequence.",
}

